  "articleBody": "Trying to install a threshold ramp yourself can seem like a quick fix, but there are some common pitfalls that can lead to frustration or, more importantly, safety hazards. One of the biggest mistakes is buying a ramp that's either too short or too steep for the rise you need to cover. This can make the ramp difficult to use and even dangerous, as it creates an abrupt incline rather than a smooth transition. Another frequent oversight is not checking the doorway clearance properly. You might end up with a ramp that obstructs the door or doesn't sit flush, creating a tripping hazard. Many folks also choose a product that doesn't quite match the threshold height or the surface conditions, leading to instability or poor grip. For example, using an indoor ramp outside means it won't stand up to weather and can become slippery. Remember, threshold ramps are typically for those smaller, 1-to-3-inch rises. If the rise is more significant or if the area is complicated, it's really best to have a professional from HH Remodeling LLC take a look. We can ensure you get the right ramp for a safe and stable solution.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}